{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The dual of l_infty may introduce finitely additive priors; measurable policy compactness may fail; the needed assumptions may be too strong to cover natural classes.","suggested_lenses":["functional-analytic minimax","compactness counterexample search","measurable policy topology"],"summary":"For infinite public-exogenous classes, investigate whether the finite-subclass modulus Phi_T equals the full grid minimax value under natural compactness or continuity assumptions. Work on fixed finite grid G_T first, define the attainable reward set in l_infty(Theta_T), and either prove a no-duality-gap theorem or construct a strict compactness-gap example.","title":"Infinite-Class No-Duality-Gap Test","why_promising":"This is the most direct route from the accepted finite-class characterization to a genuine unknown-class characterization."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The essential-sup calculation depends on conditioning on positive-probability atoms; the supremum over quotes and horizons in Delta_T(H) may expose missed contributions; bounded regret for the zero learner must be stated with the correct law-awareness and horizon convention.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample proof","tail calculation","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Formalize the solver's rare-history example: draw N with P(N=n)=c 4^{-n}, reveal a symbol s_N at time 1, then run L_N=2^n zeros followed by one spike at 1. Prove beta(h)=1 for every h, but Delta_T(H) is O(1/H) uniformly in T and the comparator value is bounded.","title":"Rare-History Beta Separation","why_promising":"It cleanly separates essential-sup raw delay tails from average value-weighted truncation, blocking a common false necessity route."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The first-hit structure across decreasing spikes is easy to mis-index; the claimed O(1/H) bound may fail for quotes near a spike threshold or for partial horizons; strict inequality P>a needs care.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic counterexample proof","value-weighted delay accounting"],"summary":"Verify or refute the strengthened tiny-price deterministic claim: blocks have L_m=2^m zeros followed by p_m=4^{-m}. Show beta(h)=1 for all finite h, while sup_T Delta_T(H)=O(1/H), not merely bounded comparator value.","title":"Tiny-Price Delta Bound","why_promising":"If correct, it gives a stronger deterministic obstruction showing even value-weighted delay loss can be small while raw beta is maximally bad."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Repeated hidden blocks can collapse because the fixed comparator averages across block types or the first reveal identifies the global type; power-tail constraints may force too little valuable mass.","suggested_lenses":["lower-bound construction","Bayes-gap computation","rate optimization"],"summary":"Build multi-scale hidden-terminal families that accumulate many qD Bayes-gap atoms without iid comparator collapse. Try nested scales D_j, probabilities q_j, and quote bands arranged so the best fixed quote remains separated across unresolved components.","title":"Multi-Scale Hidden-Terminal Lower Bounds","why_promising":"Matching the power-tail upper rate is the main missing rate result, and the one-block qD atom is known to be too weak."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Small KL or TV between terminal observations may also shrink the revenue-curve separation; after enough blocks the learner may identify the world too quickly; constants may not optimize to the desired exponent.","suggested_lenses":["Le Cam/Fano","limited-information design","posterior Bayes gap"],"summary":"Develop a limited-leakage version of repeated hidden terminals: terminal observations under different worlds have small KL/TV while their posted-price revenue curves have separated optima. Use TV/KL accumulation to lower-bound the number of unresolved rounds.","title":"Limited-Leakage Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly targets the one-reveal-learning obstruction and could turn the Bayes-gap framework into scalable lower rates."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Finite numerical examples may not scale; optimizing over priors in Psi_G can be nontrivial; observed patterns may reflect grid artifacts rather than true asymptotics.","suggested_lenses":["computational search","finite-grid exact enumeration","proof extraction"],"summary":"Use the accepted finite-grid Psi_G identity as a calculator. For small finite law classes and grids, exactly compute or optimize the least-favorable prior, compare Psi_G to delta tails, and search for families with large Bayes gap under controlled delay profiles.","title":"Psi_G Lower-Bound Calculator","why_promising":"The value identity is already proved for finite classes, so it can guide construction instead of relying on intuition about hidden delayed rewards."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The payoff a 1{a<M} is discontinuous and nonconcave; improvements may only remove logarithms; delayed feedback may force the same sqrt(T H) dependence anyway.","suggested_lenses":["upper-bound proof","posted-price learning","metric entropy"],"summary":"Investigate whether the one-dimensional posted-price structure improves the generic delayed-experts upper bound. The truncated reward functions are threshold-shaped, so chaining, continuous experts, or dynamic-pricing algorithms might reduce discretization or delay overhead.","title":"Structured Upper Bounds","why_promising":"If the existing upper rate is loose, the lower-bound target changes; this should be settled before over-investing in matching lower rates."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Closure assumptions may become artificial; predictable large delta remains harmless; constructing companion laws from a single law may be impossible without changing the model class.","suggested_lenses":["theorem formulation","necessity proof","counterexample search"],"summary":"Find natural richness conditions on unknown classes under which large value-weighted truncation loss forces a Bayes-gap lower bound. Candidate closures include mixtures, conditional variants after public histories, or hidden labels attached to delayed reward curves.","title":"When Delta Becomes Necessary","why_promising":"This could bridge the clean delta-tail sufficient theorem and the predictability-based necessary condition, yielding a scalar characterization for restricted but meaningful classes."},{"id":"idea_09","risks":"A doubling or hedging-over-horizons policy may avoid the proposed obstruction; previous-block rewards can pollute the horizon comparison; the exact anytime benchmark must be fixed first.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","horizon-free policy analysis"],"summary":"Formalize the known-law anytime obstruction suggested in the notes: a deterministic infinite sequence with super-growing blocks and two possible deadline spikes, where any horizon-free learner has large regret on one of two horizon subsequences.","title":"Anytime Known-Law Boundary","why_promising":"The known-law oracle is horizon-aware. This direction may reveal a nontrivial fixed-process regime where tail or deadline uncertainty matters."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
